--- abstract


DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) permits the owner of a signing
domain to document that they have handled an email message by
associating their domain with the message.
One part of this involves publishing a public key to a Domain Name
System (DNS) record associated with the domain. Signing with the
associated private key establishes their connection to the message.
This document describes how to find the
relevant DNS record and how information is recorded within it.

This document updates RFC 6376, which describes DKIM, by clarifying
the RSA public key format and by deprecating key record features
that have not turned out to be useful. It also describes which
parts of the key record are relevant to DomainKeys Identified Mail
v2 (DKIM2) and describes a new Domain Policy Record for DKIM2 that
can hold domain-wide settings.

--- middle

# Introduction

DomainKeys Identified Mail permits the owner of a signing
domain to document that they have handled an email message by associating
their domain with the message by means of a cryptographic signature.

[RFC6376] describes how this is achieved in a protocol we will call, for
the purposes of this document, DKIM1. [DKIM2SPEC] describes DomainKeys
Identified Mail v2, which we will call DKIM2. Usage of the term DKIM
should be understood as referring to both DKIM1 and DKIM2.

DKIM1 and DKIM2 use the same method of storing public key values in
the Domain Name System (DNS).

This document serves two purposes. It describes all the features of the
key storage system used by DKIM1 as updated by [RFC8301] and [RFC8463]
(along with errata) but then deprecates various features which have turned out
to be of little practical use. It also states which of these
features DKIM2 uses.

Further, a new DKIM2-only Domain Policy Record is defined. DKIM1
does not use this.

# Terminology and Definitions

This section defines terms used in the rest of the document.

{::boilerplate bcp14-tagged}

Terms such as Signer, Verifier, Signing Domain and Selector
are used as defined in [DKIM2SPEC]. Basic email terminology is
taken from [RFC5598].

Syntax descriptions use Augmented BNF (ABNF) [RFC5234],
together with the %s"..." notation for case-sensitive strings
defined in [RFC7405].

## Whitespace

There are two forms of whitespace used in this specification:

*  WSP represents simple whitespace, i.e. a space or a tab character
   (formal definition in [RFC5234]).

*  FWS is folding whitespace.  It allows multiple lines separated by
   CRLF followed by at least one whitespace, to be joined.

The formal ABNF for these are (WSP given for information only):

    WSP =   SP / HTAB
    FWS =   [*WSP CRLF] 1*WSP

The definition of FWS is identical to that in [RFC5322] except for
the exclusion of obs-FWS.

## Imported ABNF tokens

Tokens are imported from other RFCs as noted. Those RFCs
should be considered definitive.

* "qp-section" (a single line of quoted-printable-encoded text) is
imported from [RFC2045].

* "sub-domain" (a single domain label) is imported from [RFC5321].

Tokens not defined herein are imported from {{RFC5234}}. These are
intuitive primitives such as SP, HTAB, WSP, ALPHA, DIGIT, CRLF, etc.

## Common ABNF tokens {#common-ABNF}

The following ABNF tokens are used elsewhere in this document:

    hyphenated-word = ALPHA [ *(ALPHA / DIGIT / "-") (ALPHA / DIGIT) ]

    ALPHADIGITPS = (ALPHA / DIGIT / "+" / "/")

    base64string =  ALPHADIGITPS *([FWS] ALPHADIGITPS)
                    [ [FWS] "=" [ [FWS] "=" ] ]

Note that base64strings are defined in [RFC4648], but that document
does not contain any ABNF. A base64string MUST be padded with zero
bits and trailing = characters provided if needed. This allows
implementations to compare base64string values without decoding
them.

FWS may appear anywhere within a base64string and is ignored
when the value is used.

## Tag=Value Lists {#tagvalue}

Key records use the "tag=value" syntax defined in the "Tag=Value
Lists" section of [DKIM2SPEC], including its
rules on whitespace, case sensitivity, duplicate tags, unrecognized
tags, empty values and default values.

# Public key management

DKIM links a public key to the Signing Domain by fetching a
public key value from the DNS.

Selectors permit a Signing Domain to use multiple public keys, possibly
using different cryptographic algorithms. Selectors allow keys to
be rotated or distributed to third parties.

DKIM keys are stored in a subdomain named "_domainkey".  Given a
Signing Domain "example.com" and a Selector of "foo.bar", the DNS
query will be for "foo.bar._domainkey.example.com".

Keys are held in a TXT RR at the selector name. Strings in a TXT RR
MUST be concatenated together before use with no intervening
whitespace. TXT records that begin with a "v=" tag with a value
other than "DKIM1" are ignored. If more than one record then
remains, this is an error.

ABNF:

    selector = sub-domain *( "." sub-domain )

# Key records {#textualrepresentation}

The overall syntax is a Tag=Value list as described in {{tagvalue}}.

Key records are defined for three key types:

| Key type  | Algorithm              | DKIM1 usage                | DKIM2 usage     |
|-----------|------------------------|----------------------------|-----------------|
| "rsa"     | RSA {{RFC8017}}        | {{RFC6376}}, {{RFC8301}}   | {{DKIM2SPEC}}   |
| "ed25519" | Ed25519 {{RFC8032}}    | {{RFC8463}}                | {{DKIM2SPEC}}   |
| "mldsa44" | ML-DSA-44 {{FIPS-204}} | not defined                | {{DKIM2SPEC}}   |
{: #keytypes title="Key types"}

The algorithms used with these keys, and the RSA key sizes, are
specified in [DKIM2SPEC]; for DKIM1 see [RFC8301].

The current valid tags for key records are described below. The p=
tag MUST be present; all other tags are optional. It is RECOMMENDED
to provide a v= tag.

The h=, n=, s= and t= tags are deprecated: they SHOULD NOT be used
in DKIM1 key records, and DKIM2 ignores them if they are present.

## v= Version of the DKIM key record

If specified, this tag MUST be set to "DKIM1" (without the quotes) and
an exact match with those characters is required. If absent, the record
is processed as if v=DKIM1 were present. This tag MUST be the first tag
in the record. Records beginning with a "v=" tag with any other value
MUST be discarded.

ABNF:

    key-v-tag = %s"v" [FWS] "=" [FWS] %s"DKIM1"

## k= Key type

This tag indicates what type of key is present. If omitted then
the value "rsa" is used.

The p= tag is interpreted for the different key types as follows:

rsa: p= is an ASN.1 DER-encoded [ITU-X660-1997] RSAPublicKey
([RFC8017], Sections 3.1 and A.1.1) or a SubjectPublicKeyInfo
containing one ([RFC5280], [RFC4055]).

ed25519: p= is the 32-octet Ed25519 public key [RFC8463].

mldsa44: p= is the 1312-octet ML-DSA-44 public key output by
ML-DSA.KeyGen (Algorithm 1 of [FIPS-204], encoded by pkEncode,
Algorithm 22), with no SubjectPublicKeyInfo or other wrapper.

DKIM1 Signers and Verifiers MUST support "rsa".
DKIM2 Verifiers MUST support all three key types.

ABNF:

    key-k-tag        = %s"k" [FWS] "=" [FWS] key-k-tag-type
    key-k-tag-type   = %s"rsa" / %s"ed25519" / %s"mldsa44" /
                       x-key-k-tag-type
    x-key-k-tag-type = hyphenated-word ; for future extension

## p= Public-key data

The public key data is a base64string whose format depends on
the k= tag. An empty value means that this public key has been
revoked. An "ed25519" key that is not 32 octets, or an "mldsa44"
key that is not 1312 octets, is malformed.

ABNF:

    key-p-tag = %s"p" [FWS] "=" [ [FWS] base64string ]

## h= Acceptable hash algorithms

A colon-separated list of the hash algorithms that may be used with
this key. When absent all are allowed.

Note that [RFC8301] forbids the use of "rsa-sha1" for DKIM1.

ABNF:

    key-h-tag       = %s"h" [FWS] "=" [FWS] key-h-tag-alg
                         *( [FWS] ":" [FWS] key-h-tag-alg )
    key-h-tag-alg   = %s"sha1" / %s"sha256" / x-key-h-tag-alg
    x-key-h-tag-alg = hyphenated-word ; for future extension

## n= Notes that might be of interest to a human

The notes text MUST be in quoted-printable format. DKIM does
not use this text for any purpose.

ABNF:

    key-n-tag = %s"n" [FWS] "=" [FWS] qp-section

## s= Service type

A colon-separated list of service types to which the record
applies (default "*"). DKIM1 Verifiers ignore the key
record if the list does not contain either "*" or "email".

ABNF:

    key-s-tag        = %s"s" [FWS] "=" [FWS] key-s-tag-type
                          *( [FWS] ":" [FWS] key-s-tag-type )
    key-s-tag-type   = %s"email" / "*" / x-key-s-tag-type
    x-key-s-tag-type = hyphenated-word ; for future extension

## t= Flags

A colon-separated list of flags (default none). The flags are
"y" (the domain is testing DKIM1) and "s" (DKIM1 requires
an i= domain to be the same as d=). Unrecognized flags are
ignored.

ABNF:

    key-t-tag        = %s"t" [FWS] "=" [FWS] key-t-tag-flag
                          *( [FWS] ":" [FWS] key-t-tag-flag )
    key-t-tag-flag   = %s"y" / %s"s" / x-key-t-tag-flag
    x-key-t-tag-flag = hyphenated-word ; for future extension

# Domain Policy Record {#domain-policy}

The Domain Policy Record for a domain is a TXT RR at "_domainkey"
under that domain (for "example.com", "_domainkey.example.com").

This record is used by DKIM2 and not by DKIM1.

It is a Tag=Value list (see {{tagvalue}}). The two current valid
tags are described below. The v= tag MUST be present; the f= tag
is optional.

TXT records at that name that do not begin with v=DKIM1 are
ignored. If more than one remains, this is an error.

If the "strict" flag is absent, or there is no f= tag, or
there is no Domain Policy Record, subdomains are authorized.

The record applies only to the domain that holds it; it is not
inherited by subdomains or parents.

## v= Version of the Domain Policy Record

This tag MUST be set to "DKIM1" (without the quotes) and
an exact match with those characters is required.
This tag MUST be the first tag in the record.

ABNF:

    dpr-v-tag = %s"v" [FWS] "=" [FWS] %s"DKIM1"

## f= Flags for the Domain Policy Record

A colon-separated list of flags (default none). One flag is
defined, "strict": subdomains of the domain are not authorized
to make From header fields that use the domain Authentic (see
[DKIM2SPEC]). Unrecognized flags are ignored.

ABNF:

    dpr-f-tag        = %s"f" [FWS] "=" [ [FWS] dpr-f-tag-flag
                          *( [FWS] ":" [FWS] dpr-f-tag-flag ) ]
    dpr-f-tag-flag   = %s"strict" / x-dpr-f-tag-flag
    x-dpr-f-tag-flag = hyphenated-word ; for future extension

# Updates to RFC 6376

This document updates [RFC6376] as follows:

- The h=, n=, s= and t= tags are deprecated (see {{textualrepresentation}}).

- An "rsa" key may be either an RSAPublicKey or a
  SubjectPublicKeyInfo containing one. This resolves errata
  3017, 6674 and 7001.

- TXT records at a selector name that begin with v= other
  than "DKIM1" are ignored; more than one remaining record is
  an error.

# IANA Considerations

IANA is requested to make the following changes to registries in
the "DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) Parameters" group. As set out
in [RFC6376], the status of an entry is either "active" or
"historic".

## _domainkey DNS TXT Record Tag Specifications

IANA is requested to update the following entries and add the "f"
entry. Other entries are unchanged.

| TYPE | REFERENCE           | STATUS   |
|------|---------------------|----------|
| v    | [RFC6376], RFC XXXX | active   |
| h    | [RFC6376], RFC XXXX | historic |
| k    | [RFC6376], RFC XXXX | active   |
| n    | [RFC6376], RFC XXXX | historic |
| p    | [RFC6376], RFC XXXX | active   |
| s    | [RFC6376], RFC XXXX | historic |
| t    | [RFC6376], RFC XXXX | historic |
| f    | RFC XXXX            | active   |

IANA is also requested to add a note to the registry: "Tags in this
registry are used in DKIM key records and in the DKIM2 Domain
Policy Record. A tag name is registered once and has one meaning."

